Z||000022||FieroKyle||07-17-2001||03:30 PM||||Well, I did this and it works great. I just put the seatbelt in back of the seat and buckled it in. You can barely tell I did this and the flapping stopped for now. Thanks for the ideas and stories.<P>Kyle - 85' GT / Lisence Plate #: TICKET||||reg||1
Z||000023||lawrence||07-17-2001||08:47 PM||||I have one of those little plates that holds the belt out a little ways.  Do you know what I mean?  The plate came from a child seat.  It is used to hold the belt so that it won't come out with the seat in it.  It looks like a modified "H".<P>I pulled the belt out about 4" and put the plate on the belt to hold it out.  With no tension on it, it doesn't flap.<P>Good luck,<P>-L<BR> <A HREF="http://members.nccw.net/webe/products" TARGET=_blank>http://members.nccw.net/webe/products</A>  - WebElectric Products||||reg||1
Z||000024||tgowens||07-17-2001||08:57 PM||||I use one of the large size "chip clips" (for potato chip bags) and it keeps the belt from flapping..............(it does make me hungry on long trips tho when I realize that there are no chips with the clip!)||||reg||1
Z||000025||terryk||07-17-2001||10:05 PM||||Eric, doesn't West Coast Fiero's have a solution for this?||||reg||1
